---
import ToolPage from '../layouts/ToolPage.astro';
import CropImage from '../tools/CropImage';
import { getTool } from '../data/tools';

const tool = getTool('crop-image');
---

<ToolPage tool={tool}>
  <CropImage slot="tool" client:load />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="exact-dimensions">Getting exactly the dimensions you asked for</h2>
      <p>
        Most crop tools give you a rectangle with draggable corners and report the size as you
        drag. Getting to precisely 1080 by 1080 that way is fiddly on a mouse and close to
        impossible on a phone, and being one pixel out is exactly the kind of thing an upload
        validator rejects.
      </p>
      <p>
        This tool works the other way round. You type the output size you need, the frame locks
        to that shape, and you move the image behind it. The output is exactly the dimensions you
        entered because it cannot be anything else — there is no dragging accuracy involved.
      </p>
      <p>
        Drag to reposition, scroll or pinch to zoom. The rule-of-thirds guides are there to help
        you place the subject; the frame edge is where the cut happens.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="crop-vs-resize">Cropping and resizing are different operations</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Cropping</strong> cuts pixels away from the edges. What remains keeps its
        original size and detail. Use it when the image is the wrong shape, or when there is too
        much empty space around the subject.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Resizing</strong> keeps the whole picture but scales every pixel. Use it when the
        shape is right but the image is too large or too small.
      </p>
      <p>
        The two are often needed together: crop to the right shape, then scale to the right size.
        That is what the "scale to these exact dimensions" option does — it crops the framed area
        and then scales the result to your target. Turn it off if you would rather keep the
        cropped region at its original pixel density.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you only need to change the size, the <a href="/resize-image/">resizer</a> is the
        simpler tool.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="ratios">Which aspect ratio for which platform</h2>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Ratio</th>
              <th>Common size</th>
              <th>Where it is used</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>1:1</td>
              <td>1080 x 1080</td>
              <td>Instagram squares, marketplace listing photos, avatars</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>4:5</td>
              <td>1080 x 1350</td>
              <td>Instagram portrait posts — the most feed space you can occupy</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>16:9</td>
              <td>1920 x 1080</td>
              <td>YouTube thumbnails, video, presentation slides</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>9:16</td>
              <td>1080 x 1920</td>
              <td>Stories, Reels, TikTok, Shorts</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>2:3</td>
              <td>1000 x 1500</td>
              <td>Pinterest pins — taller pins get truncated</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>3:2</td>
              <td>1800 x 1200</td>
              <td>Standard photographic print sizes such as 6 x 4</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Need several of these from one image? The{' '}
        <a href="/social-media-image-sizes/">social media sizer</a> produces them all at once.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="quality">Cropping and image quality</h2>
      <p>
        Cropping itself is lossless — the remaining pixels are untouched. What costs a little
        quality is saving the result: a JPG has to be re-encoded, which is one more generation of
        compression. It is a small effect, but it is a reason to crop from your original rather
        than from a copy that has already been compressed several times.
      </p>
      <p>
        The bigger risk is cropping too tightly. Cutting a small region out of a small image
        leaves you with a small image, and enlarging it back up will not restore the detail. Keep
        an eye on the output dimensions shown below the frame — if the tool warns that the framed
        area is smaller than your target size, it is about to enlarge and soften your crop.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolPage>
